ONE ACTUAL THUMB BRANCH LISTING TRANSLATED FROM THE BOTTOM UPWARD.

Status: initial source-derived trace specimen; not a compiler and not
a claim that these machine instructions were executed in this job.

Original external source:
Android Open Source Project, ART, pinned revision
08490b84048a0267694268185441b70cfa090185,
compiler/utils/assembler_thumb_test_expected.cc.inc,
array named SimpleBranchResults.
https://android.googlesource.com/platform/art/+/08490b84048a0267694268185441b70cfa090185/compiler/utils/assembler_thumb_test_expected.cc.inc

The external file provides an expected ARM Thumb assembler listing.
The original source's offset labels and observed halfword encodings
are evidence of a test expectation checked into ART. They are NOT
evidence that we built, executed or reassembled that test here.
This experiment does not reconstruct an R function or invent a
high-level source. It starts with encoded machine instructions.

Architecture assumed for the English interpretation:
ARMv7-A in T32 instruction state, unprivileged, with the
current closed 73-operation profile in the architecture directory.
This is a valid target choice for the *interpretation*;
no concrete mobile CPU was observed during this work.

PART ONE. THE RELEVANT ORIGINAL ENCODINGS.

The following pairs give an offset relative to the original
fixture's instruction-stream start, followed by the 16-bit
instruction halfword as a hexadecimal number.

    Offset 0x0006: 0xE000
    Offset 0x0008: 0x2102
    Offset 0x000A: 0x2003
    Offset 0x000C: 0x2002
    Offset 0x000E: 0x2101
    Offset 0x0010: 0xD0FD
    Offset 0x0012: 0xD000
    Offset 0x0014: 0x2102
    Offset 0x0016: 0x2003
    Offset 0x0018: 0xE002
    Offset 0x001A: 0x2104
    Offset 0x001C: 0xE000
    Offset 0x001E: 0x2105
    Offset 0x0020: 0x2006

For this specimen, interpret these numbers as actual T32
instruction halfwords, not as a single array of integer data
that has somehow already been executed.

The four relevant machine instruction families are:
  MOVS (immediate): 16-bit T32 form setting N and Z;
  B (unconditional): 16-bit direct PC-relative branch;
  B with condition EQ: 16-bit branch only when Z=1;
  (other instructions in the published fixture exist,
   but this specimen does not expand them).

The original disassembly renders one of the branches as
"b.n", one as "beq.n", and one of the register writes as
"movs". These short assembler names remain provenance, not
the chosen English Major C operation names.

PART TWO. A PRECISE DECLARATION OF THE STARTING STATE.

Choose the execution index to be offset 0x0006.
Assume every listed instruction address is executable in
the selected T32 state, and all listed bytes are present
and identically visible to the instruction fetch mechanism.
The 16-bit encodings are well-formed at the assumed address.
The value of each general register initially is unknown
unless assigned by one of the listed operations.
The initial values of N, Z, C, V are unknown.
The values of SP and LR are unknown and are NOT assumed
to contain a valid call frame or return location.
No instruction in the traced path reads or writes data memory.
For this trace, no exception, asynchronous interrupt,
or external concurrent modification is introduced.

Offsets in this file are relative, not independently observed
operating-system virtual addresses. To execute at a concrete
address, a loader must supply a base and a matching executable
mapping. Do not treat a listing offset as the actual program
counter value in a process that has never been launched.

PART THREE. THE MACHINE-LEVEL TRACE EXPRESSED AS ENGLISH.

STEP ONE.

The execution index is 0x0006 relative to the known code base.
The halfword has value 0xE000, a 16-bit unconditional
T32 branch with an immediate displacement of zero.
Apply the operation whose full long English name is
"Replace the execution index with the named legal T32
instruction address without modifying the link register."
The architectural PC-relative base of this instruction
is 0x000A. Adding the encoded displacement zero produces
the selected successor offset 0x000A.

No register, memory location, or condition flag changes.
The 16-bit halfword at offset 0x0008 is therefore NOT
executed in this trace. Its bytes have not disappeared:
they remain ordinary contents of the same instruction
stream and might be decoded on another execution path.

STEP TWO.

The execution index is 0x000A. The halfword 0x2003 is
the 16-bit MOVS-immediate encoding that chooses register
R0 and unsigned literal 3. Apply operation 53, whose
name states that it writes the zero-extended eight-bit
literal to a low general-purpose register and updates
negative and zero flags without changing carry or
overflow. After this step:
    R0 is exactly 0x00000003;
    N is zero;
    Z is zero;
    C and V retain their prior unknown values.
The next sequential index is offset 0x000C.

STEP THREE.

At offset 0x000C, halfword 0x2002 selects the same
operation 53 with R0 and literal 2. This overwrites
the old value of R0. After this step:
    R0 is exactly 0x00000002;
    N is zero;
    Z is zero;
    C and V retain their prior values.
The next index is offset 0x000E.

STEP FOUR.

At offset 0x000E, halfword 0x2101 selects operation
53 with R1 and literal 1. After the step:
    R1 is exactly 0x00000001;
    R0 remains 0x00000002;
    N is zero;
    Z is zero;
    C and V remain unchanged from their prior values.
The next index is offset 0x0010.

STEP FIVE.

At offset 0x0010, halfword 0xD0FD is a conditional
branch requesting a branch on EQUAL, meaning Z=1.
Its 8-bit signed displacement field has bit pattern
11111101 and signed integer value negative three;
multiplying by two yields negative six bytes.
The architectural PC operand base at offset
0x0010 is offset 0x0014, and negative six
added to 0x0014 gives target offset 0x000E.

In the particular trace begun at offset 0x0006,
Z is zero because of the MOVS at offset 0x000E.
Therefore the condition fails. This instruction
does NOT take its target at 0x000E.
It changes neither the general registers nor
the condition flags.
It advances the execution index by its actual
16-bit length, to offset 0x0012.

STEP SIX.

At offset 0x0012, halfword 0xD000 is another
EQUAL conditional branch. Its immediate displacement
is zero and its architectural PC-relative base is
offset 0x0016, which is therefore its *taken* target.

However, the current Z flag is still zero.
Consequently the branch is NOT taken and ordinary
sequential execution proceeds to offset 0x0014.
The coincidence that its taken target would have
been offset 0x0016 does not allow the description
to claim that the branch executed.

STEP SEVEN.

At offset 0x0014, halfword 0x2102 writes unsigned
literal 2 into R1 through operation 53.
After the step, R1 is 0x00000002, Z=0 and N=0.
The next index is offset 0x0016.

STEP EIGHT.

At offset 0x0016, halfword 0x2003 writes unsigned
literal 3 into R0 through operation 53.
After the step, R0 is 0x00000003, Z=0 and N=0.
The next index is offset 0x0018.

STEP NINE.

At offset 0x0018, halfword 0xE002 performs a 16-bit
unconditional branch with immediate field value 2.
Its architectural PC-relative base is offset
0x001C, and its displacement is two times 2
bytes, giving target offset 0x0020.
The execution index becomes offset 0x0020.
The instructions located at offsets 0x001A,
0x001C and 0x001E are skipped by THIS trace.
Their bytes are still part of the source listing.

STEP TEN.

At offset 0x0020, halfword 0x2006 writes unsigned
literal 6 into R0 through operation 53.
After the step, R0 is 0x00000006, R1 remains
0x00000002, N is zero and Z is zero;
C and V retain their unknown original values.
Its sequential successor is offset 0x0022.

The provided listing excerpt ends at offset 0x0020.
The English translation MUST STOP at the unresolved
next-instruction boundary. It must NOT invent a
return instruction, a function result, a process exit,
or an additional R function call at 0x0022.

PART FOUR. AN ALTERNATIVE ENTRY SHOWING A TAKEN CONDITION.

Choose a separate initial state with execution index
0x0010, Z=1, and an otherwise permissible processor
state. This is NOT the same run as the first trace.

At 0x0010, the EQUAL branch is taken to 0x000E,
because the entering value of Z is one.

At 0x000E, operation 53 writes literal 1 to R1
and sets Z=0. Execution advances to 0x0010.

At 0x0010, the SAME branch encoding is encountered
again, but it is NOT taken because Z is now zero.
Execution proceeds to 0x0012.

This makes the dependence on the flag-producing
instruction concrete. Replacing MOVS with a
flag-preserving MOV would change this trace for
some initial flag states, even though the same
integer 1 reaches R1. Therefore the distinction
between operations 02 and 53 is semantically
necessary, not cosmetic.

PART FIVE. INERT REASONING THAT MUST SURVIVE.

(NOTE:
    The source of byte encodings is a pinned AOSP
    checked-in assembler expectation, not a live
    executable from a user device. The chosen entry
    at offset 0x0006 is the start of an isolated
    segment, not the function's published entry
    point. Starting at the fixture's offset zero
    would encounter an earlier backward branch
    that may not reach the segment in this trace.
)

(WHY? BECAUSE:
    The purpose of this fixture is to check the
    translation of machine control flow before
    inferring any high-level program structure.
    The source-level statements "if", "for",
    "loop" or "return" would be a later
    interpretation, not a substitute for the
    machine's actual branches and register effects.
)

PART SIX. ACCEPTANCE AND REJECTION.

An independent decoder of the actual halfwords
should confirm all listed widths and targets.

An English Major C semantic interpreter, when one
exists, must reproduce:
    trace A successor offsets, in order:
      0x0006, 0x000A, 0x000C, 0x000E, 0x0010,
      0x0012, 0x0014, 0x0016, 0x0018, 0x0020,
      and then unresolved 0x0022;
    trace A final known R0=6, R1=2, Z=0, N=0;
    trace B's two visits to 0x0010 with different
      Z values and correspondingly different outcomes.

Deliberately defective versions must be rejected
if they: execute the 0x0008 halfword in trace A;
replace the 0xD0FD signed displacement by an
unsigned +253 displacement; change MOVS to MOV;
treat 0x0010 as having length four; treat a
branch's PC-relative base as its next sequential
address; claim C and V were overwritten by MOVS;
or invent an instruction at 0x0022.

These acceptance cases have been SPECIFIED here,
not run against an English Major C implementation.
The disassembler expectation is external evidence
for these halfwords, not proof of independent
English-language parser/executor functionality.
